the pro rata calculation?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. Roth IRAs do not count in the pro rata calculation. Line 6 asks only for the value of your traditional IRAs, which the instructions define to include SEP and SIMPLE IRAs. Existing balances in Roth IRAs are excluded from the denominator."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I fix the pro rata tax after I convert?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No, you cannot undo a conversion after it occurs. Under IRS Publication 590-A, conversions made after 2017 cannot be recharacterized. You can lower the tax before December 31 by rolling your pre-tax balances into a workplace plan that accepts them, but you cannot fix the calculation after year end."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why does the calculator add my converted amount to my December 31 balance?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The IRS formula requires you to measure your total IRA value before the conversion occurred. Adding line 8 back to line 6 reconstructs the full year-end value of your accounts. The formula then divides your basis by this combined total to determine the tax-free share."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What happens if my IRAs hold only after-tax money?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Your conversion is entirely tax-free. When your basis equals your total IRA value, the calculated nontaxable share is 1.000. That fraction leaves zero taxable dollars on line 18, and any remaining basis carries forward to the following tax year."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is Form 8606 line 6?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Line 6 is the total value of all your traditional, SEP, and SIMPLE IRAs as of December 31, plus outstanding rollovers. An outstanding rollover is generally a distribution received after November 1 that is rolled over within 60 days in the following year. Outstanding rollovers to qualified retirement plans are excluded."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I lower my pre-tax IRA balance before December 31?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"You can lower your pre-tax IRA balance by rolling it into an employer qualified retirement plan before December 31. Under IRS Publication 590-A, qualified plans may accept rollovers from traditional IRAs, but they are not required to do so. Readers evaluating workplace options can also review our guide on the mega backdoor Roth 401(k)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does this calculator include state tax?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. The calculator models federal rules only. State and local income taxes vary by jurisdiction and are outside the scope of this tool. You must calculate any applicable state income taxes separately."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is this tax advice?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. This page provides general federal tax information based on the tax year 2025 Form 8606 instructions. It is not personal tax advice. A qualified tax professional can help you apply these rules to your specific tax return."}}]}</script><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Person","name":"Jonathan Velez","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/about/","jobTitle":"Personal Finance Writer & Editor","description":"Jonathan Velez writes and edits ModernWallet's personal-finance content, focused on loans, interest, and debt payoff. Every calculator page is reviewed against primary sources like the CFPB and Federal Reserve.","knowsAbout":["Auto loans","Loan amortization","Interest and APR","Debt payoff strategies","Personal finance"],"worksFor":{"@type":"Organization","name":"ModernWallet","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com"}}</script> <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","headline":"Backdoor Roth Pro Rata Rule Calculator","description":"Calculate how much of your backdoor Roth conversion is taxable under the IRS pro rata rule. See Form 8606 lines, tax-free basis, and December 31 balance math.","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/backdoor-roth-pro-rata-calculator/","image":{"@type":"ImageObject","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/favicon.svg"},"datePublished":"2026-11-18","dateModified":"2026-11-18","author":{"@type":"Person","name":"Jonathan Velez","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/about/"},"publisher":{"@type":"Organization","name":"ModernWallet","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com","logo":{"@type":"ImageObject","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/favicon.svg"}},"citation":[{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"IRS Instructions for Form 8606 (tax year 2025)","url":"https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i8606"},{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"IRS About Form 8606","url":"https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-8606"},{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"IRS Publication 590-A, Contributions to Individual Retirement Arrangements (IRAs)","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p590a"},{"@type":"CreativeWork","name":"IRS Publication 590-B, Distributions from Individual Retirement Arrangements (IRAs)","url":"https://www.irs.gov/publications/p590b"}]}</script> <main class="page" data-astro-cid-5nhdsx2r> <nav class="crumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb" data-astro-cid-5nhdsx2r> <a href="/" data-astro-cid-5nhdsx2r>ModernWallet</a> <span data-astro-cid-5nhdsx2r>/</span> <span class="crumb-current" data-astro-cid-5nhdsx2r>Backdoor Roth Pro Rata</span> </nav> <header class="hero" data-astro-cid-5nhdsx2r> <h1 data-astro-cid-5nhdsx2r>Backdoor Roth Pro Rata Rule Calculator</h1> <div class="byline" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr> <span class="by" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>By <a href="/about/" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>Jonathan Velez</a>, Personal Finance Writer &amp; Editor</span> <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>·</span> <span class="rev" data-astro-cid-oxbng3yr>Reviewed against primary sources · Updated November 18, 2026</span> </div>   <p class="lede" data-astro-cid-5nhdsx2r>Your taxable conversion share equals one minus your total basis divided by the sum of your December 31 IRA value and the amount you converted. The converted dollars are added into the bottom of that fraction. The proportion measures your balances across all traditional, Simplified Employee Pension (SEP), and Savings Incentive Match Plan for Employees (SIMPLE) individual retirement arrangements (IRAs).</p><p class="lede" data-astro-cid-5nhdsx2r>For example, suppose you make a $7,500 nondeductible contribution and convert the entire $7,500. If you still hold $92,500 in other traditional IRAs on December 31, your tax-free share is 0.075. That calculation leaves $562.50 tax-free and makes $6,937.50 taxable. That means 92.5% of your conversion is taxable.</p> </header> <div class="tool-wrap" data-astro-cid-5nhdsx2r> <button type="button" class="share-btn" data-share-title="Backdoor Roth Pro Rata Rule Calculator" aria-label="Share this tool" data-astro-cid-qan2qojn> <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true" data-astro-cid-qan2qojn><path d="M12 3v12M7.5 7.5 12 3l4.5 4.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" data-astro-cid-qan2qojn></path><path d="M5 12v7a1 1 0 0 0 1 1h12a1 1 0 0 0 1-1v-7" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" data-astro-cid-qan2qojn></path></svg> <span class="share-label" data-astro-cid-qan2qojn>Share</span> </button> <span class="share-status" role="status" aria-live="polite" data-astro-cid-qan2qojn></span> <script>
